"a8687a", "text": "<math><mn>9</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec922"}, {"id": "6132c1", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec923"}, {"id": "2ceaf3", "text": "<math><mn>18</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec924"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec925", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a5"], "question": {"text": "Width of one of the two slits in a Young's double slit interference experiment is half of the other slit. The ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern is :"}, "options": [{"id": "8bf657", "text": "$(2 \\sqrt{2}+1):(2 \\sqrt{2}-1)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec926"}, {"id": "4d9d64", "text": "$(3+2 \\sqrt{2}):(3-2 \\sqrt{2})$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec927"}, {"id": "b8d23b", "text": "$9: 1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec928"}, {"id": "25f2e5", "text": "$3: 1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec929"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df1"], "question": {"text": "An unpolarised light beam of intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is passed through a polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and then through another polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> which is oriented in such a way that its passing axis makes an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> relative to that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>. The intensity of the emergent light is"}, "options": [{"id": "923210", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92b"}, {"id": "f8f57f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92c"}, {"id": "56377d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92d"}, {"id": "ea056f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec92f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements :<br />Statement I : If the Brewster&#39;s angle for the light propagating from air to glass is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#952;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math>, then Brewster&#39;s angle for the light propagating from glass to air is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>&#952;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math>.</p><p>Statement II : The Brewster&#39;s angle for the light propagating from glass to air is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub></mfenced></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub></math> is the refractive index of glass.<br />In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "455de3", "text": "Both Statements I and Statement II are true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec930"}, {"id": "19c86d", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec931"}, {"id": "284b9b", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec932"}, {"id": "dccf87", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec933"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec934", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef4"], "question": {"text": "In a Young&#39;s double slit experiment, light of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> is used to produce and interference pattern. When the distance between the slits is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mi>mm</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the angular width (in degree) of the fringes formed on the distance screen is close to :"}, "options": [{"id": "d381e3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec935"}, {"id": "435fbf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>57</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec936"}, {"id": "704107", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec937"}, {"id": "26d3e1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>07</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec938"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec939", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f993e"], "question": {"text": "A light whose electric field vectors are completely removed by using a good polaroid, allowed to incident on the surface of the prism at Brewster&#39;s angle. Choose the most suitable option for the phenomenon related to the prism."}, "options": [{"id": "f9d938", "text": "Reflected and refracted rays will be perpendicular to each other.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93a"}, {"id": "acd644", "text": "Wave will propagate along the surface of prism.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93b"}, {"id": "b3151f", "text": "No refraction, and there will be total reflection of light.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93c"}, {"id": "a62af5", "text": "No reflection. and there will be total transmission of light.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "A microwave of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> falls normally on a slit of width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The angular spread of the central maxima of the diffraction pattern obtained on a screen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> away from the slit, will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "246251", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>º</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec93f"}, {"id": "123683", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>º</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec940"}, {"id": "49b585", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>º</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec941"}, {"id": "2b8a68", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>º</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec942"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec943", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b470d"], "question": {"text": "In Young&#39;s double slit arrangement, slits are separated by a gap of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>, and the screen is placed at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> from them. The distance between the first and the third bright fringe formed when the slits are illuminated by a monochromatic light of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5890</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mover></math>&nbsp;is :-"}, "options": [{"id": "dd83d8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1178</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec944"}, {"id": "5d791e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1178</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec945"}, {"id": "f57494", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1178</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec946"}, {"id": "aff3aa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5890</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec947"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec948", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f993e"], "question": {"text": "In a Young's double slit experiment, three polarizers are kept as shown in the figure. The transmission axes of $P_1$ and $P_2$ are orthogonal to each other. The polarizer $P_3$ covers both the slits with its transmission axis at $45^{\\circ}$ to those of $P_1$ and $P_2$. An unpolarized light of wavelength $\\lambda$ and intensity $I_0$ is incident on $P_1$ and $P_2$. The intensity at a point after $P_3$ where the path difference between the light waves from $s_1$ and $s_2$ is $\\frac{\\lambda}{3}$, is\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F9S6x01tQt9218voaYBlmkPcncEfSCAcGNF4nk13x-CM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>"}, "options": [{"id": "50494f", "text": "$\\frac{I_0}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec949"}, {"id": "f69a75", "text": "$\\frac{I_0}{4}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94a"}, {"id": "b41fbd", "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_0}{3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94b"}, {"id": "50a8d6", "text": "$I_0$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95c8"], "question": {"text": "In young&#39;s double slit experiment, the fringe width is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. If the entire arrangement is placed in water of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>, then the fringe width becomes (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>mm</mi></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "8df722", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94e"}, {"id": "fd9f0f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec94f"}, {"id": "c5259f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec950"}, {"id": "66a6fa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec951"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec952", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f993e"], "question": {"text": "Two polarisers $P_1$ and $P_2$ are placed in such a way that the intensity of the transmitted light will be zero. A third polariser $P_3$ is inserted in between $P_1$ and $\\mathrm{P}_2$, at the particular angle between $\\mathrm{P}_2$ and $\\mathrm{P}_3$. The transmitted intensity of the light passing the through all three polarisers is maximum. The angle between the polarisers $\\mathrm{P}_2$ and $\\mathrm{P}_3$ is :"}, "options": [{"id": "b3362d", "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec953"}, {"id": "06315c", "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec954"}, {"id": "ce9942", "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{8}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec955"}, {"id": "562ff6", "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec956"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec957", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a5"], "question": {"text": "In Young's double slit experiment, light from two identical sources are superimposing on a screen. The path difference between the two lights reaching at a point on the screen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. The ratio of intensity of fringe at this point with respect to the maximum intensity of the fringe is:"}, "options": [{"id": "e89fc5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec958"}, {"id": "76154d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec959"}, {"id": "49b91d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95a"}, {"id": "958f10", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef4"], "question": {"text": "The width of fringe is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> on the screen in a double slit experiment for the light of wavelength of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math>. The width of the fringe for the light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "dcf8a7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95d"}, {"id": "bbbc8c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95e"}, {"id": "a685a0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>33</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec95f"}, {"id": "9902f7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec960"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec961", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b470d"], "question": {"text": "In a Young&#39;s double slit experiment slit separation <math><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo></math> one observes a bright fringe at angle <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></math> by using light of wavelength <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math> When the light of wavelength <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> is used a bright fringe is seen at the same angle in the same set up. Given that <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> are in visible range <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>380</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>740</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo></math> their values are:"}, "options": [{"id": "d8cfc8", "text": "<math><mn>400</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi> </mi><mn>500</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec962"}, {"id": "0d4ad2", "text": "<math><mn>380</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi> </mi><mn>525</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec963"}, {"id": "7f5a14", "text": "<math><mn>625</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi> </mi><mn>500</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec964"}, {"id": "d42196", "text": "<math><mn>380</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi> </mi><mn>500</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec965"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4eefc677dc36a88ec966", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95c8"], "question": {"text": "In young&#39;s double slit experiment performed using a monochromatic light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, when a glass plate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> of thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></math> is int